(2) Number the particles of the population k = 1, set the population size to kmax, the
typical time series scenario n = 1, and the daily time period t = 1;
(3) Calculate the probabilistic load flow at time period t based on multi-scenario under
a typical time series case n;
(4) Judge whether the constraints are satisfied or not; if not, make the fitness corre-
sponding to f 1 and f 4 Cmax, and the fitness corresponding to f 2f 3 Cmax, and go to step (8);
if satisfied, go to step (5);
(5) Judge whether t < 48 is valid; if so, make t = t + 1 and go to step (3); otherwise, go
to step (6);
(6) Judge whether n < 12 is valid; if so, make n = n + 1 and t = 1, go to step (3);
otherwise, go to step (7);
(7) Calculate the degree of adaptation based on the lower-level objective function;
(8) Judge whether k < kmax is valid; if so, make k = k + 1 and n = t = 1, go to step (3);
otherwise, go to step (9);
(9) Select the individual optimal solution: judge the dominance relationship before
and after updating each particle; if there is a dominance relationship, select the dominant
particle as the optimal individual, otherwise choose one randomly;
(10) Form a new Pareto non-dominated solution set: merge the new solution set with
the old Pareto non-dominated solution set, and then filter and de-duplicate to form a new
Pareto non-dominated set;
(11) Judge whether I < Imax is valid; if so, make I = I + 1 and go to step (12); otherwise,
go to step (14);
(12) Select the global optimal solution: select the population optimal particles from
the Pareto non-dominated set based on the small niching sharing technique;
(13) Update all particles and return to step (2);
(14) Select the compromise optimal solution based on fuzzy affiliation and variance
assignment.
